<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Korea through the Ages</h1><p class="path-tagline">Follow the Korean Peninsula from its early farming villages to its division in 1945.</p><p class="path-intro">Archaeologists name a long stretch of Korean prehistory after the plain pottery of the Mumun farmers, whose villages gave way to the first chiefdom-like societies in Korea. Gojoseon fell to the Han in 108 BCE, and the Three Kingdoms of Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla contended for the peninsula until Silla, allied with Tang China, destroyed its rivals and then expelled the Tang. Goryeo reunified the peninsula in 936 and gave Korea its Western name; Joseon, its longest-ruling dynasty, made Neo-Confucianism the basis of the state and created the Korean alphabet. The path ends with the Japanese protectorate of 1905 and the division of 1945, which left two Korean states.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/mumun-culture"><span class="path-step-ph">1</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 1500 BCE – c. 300 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mumun culture</span><span class="path-step-why">Named after its plain pottery, the culture of c. 1500–300 BCE saw intensive farming, large villages and the first chiefdom-like societies in Korea.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ugeo-of-gojoseon"><span class="path-step-ph">2</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · ?–108 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ugeo of Gojoseon</span><span class="path-step-why">The last king of Wiman Joseon, the final phase of Gojoseon, refused to submit to Emperor Wu of Han, whose invasion took his capital in 108 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/goguryeo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 37 BCE – 668 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Goguryeo</span><span class="path-step-why">The largest of the Three Kingdoms ruled Manchuria and the northern peninsula, and its fortresses held off Sui and Tang invasions for seventy years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/baekje"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asuka-dera.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 18 BCE – 660 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Baekje</span><span class="path-step-why">The south-western kingdom, known for refined Buddhist art, passed Chinese writing, Buddhism and craftsmen to Yamato Japan before falling to Silla and the Tang in 660.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/silla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulguksa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 57 BCE – 935 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Silla</span><span class="path-step-why">Allied with Tang China, Silla destroyed Baekje and Goguryeo, expelled the Tang by 676 and presided over a Buddhist golden age at Gyeongju.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/goryeo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gung-ye.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 918 CE – 1392 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Goryeo</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded by Wang Geon in 918, the dynasty that gave Korea its Western name carved the Tripitaka Koreana and endured Mongol overlordship from 1259.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/joseon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1392 CE – 1897 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Joseon</span><span class="path-step-why">Korea's longest-ruling dynasty, founded by Yi Seong-gye in 1392, made Neo-Confucianism the basis of state and society and survived Japanese and Manchu invasions.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/hangul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hangul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Term · 1443</span><span class="path-step-name">Hangul</span><span class="path-step-why">Created in 1443 under King Sejong and promulgated as 'the proper sounds for instructing the people', the alphabet became Korea's national script in the 20th century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1905</span><span class="path-step-name">Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905</span><span class="path-step-why">Pushed through with troops around the palace after the Russo-Japanese War, the treaty made Korea a Japanese protectorate; Japan annexed the country in 1910.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/division-of-korea"><span class="path-step-ph">10</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1945</span><span class="path-step-name">Division of Korea</span><span class="path-step-why">Split at the 38th parallel in 1945, the peninsula gained two rival states in 1948, and the armistice of 1953 left it divided along the Demilitarized Zone.</span></span></a></li></ol>
